// ============================================================================
// KQL Basics — Your First Queries in Azure Log Analytics
// ============================================================================
//
// WHAT YOU WILL LEARN:
//   - How to run a basic KQL query
//   - The most common KQL operators: take, where, project, sort, count
//   - How to explore the tables that Microsoft Foundry writes to
//   - How to filter and shape your results
//
// WHERE TO RUN THESE QUERIES:
//   Open the Azure Portal → Navigate to your Log Analytics workspace
//   (the one connected to your Application Insights resource) → Click "Logs"
//   Copy-paste each query block below into the query editor and click "Run"
//
// TIP: Run each query one at a time. Select just the lines you want to run,
//      then click "Run". This way you can step through the demo.
// ============================================================================


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: See what tables are available
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// When Microsoft Foundry sends telemetry to Application Insights, the data
// lands in several tables. Let's start by simply looking at one of them.
//
// The "take" operator grabs a specified number of rows — great for exploring.

AppRequests
| take 5

// You should see 5 rows from the AppRequests table. Each row represents
// an incoming request that your Foundry application handled.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Explore another table — AppDependencies
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// AppDependencies stores outbound calls your app made. When your Foundry
// agent calls an AI model (like gpt-5.1), that call shows up here.

AppDependencies
| take 5

// Look at the columns: Name, Target, DurationMs, Success, Properties.
// These will be very important in the next demos.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: The "where" operator — Filtering rows
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The "where" operator keeps only the rows that match a condition.
// Let's find only the requests from the last 24 hours.

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| take 10

// "ago(24h)" means "24 hours before now". You can also use:
//   ago(1h)   — last hour
//   ago(7d)   — last 7 days
//   ago(30m)  — last 30 minutes


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Combining filters
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// You can chain multiple "where" clauses. Let's find only successful
// requests from the last 24 hours.

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| where Success == true
| take 10

// You can also combine conditions in a single "where" using "and":
AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h) and Success == true
| take 10


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: The "project" operator — Choosing which columns to show
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Tables have many columns. Use "project" to pick only the ones you need.
// This makes results easier to read.

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| project TimeGenerated, Name, DurationMs, Success, ResultCode
| take 10

// Now you see just 5 clean columns instead of the full wide table.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: The "sort" operator — Ordering your results
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's find the slowest requests. We sort by DurationMs in descending order.

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| project TimeGenerated, Name, DurationMs, Success, ResultCode
| sort by DurationMs desc
| take 10

// The slowest request is now at the top. Change "desc" to "asc" for
// the fastest requests instead.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: The "count" operator — How many rows are there?
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// "count" tells you the total number of rows that match your filters.

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| count

// This gives you a single number: how many requests happened in the last day.


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: Quick look at all the main Foundry tables
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Let's check how many records exist in each of the key tables.
// Run each query separately:

// How many incoming requests?
AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| count

// How many outbound dependency calls (model calls, tool calls)?
AppDependencies
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| count

// How many log messages / traces?
AppTraces
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| count

// How many exceptions / errors?
AppExceptions
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| count


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: The "extend" operator — Adding a calculated column
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// "extend" creates a new column based on existing data.
// Let's convert DurationMs to seconds for readability.

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(24h)
| extend DurationSeconds = DurationMs / 1000.0
| project TimeGenerated, Name, DurationMs, DurationSeconds, Success
| sort by DurationSeconds desc
| take 10


// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 10: The "distinct" operator — Unique values
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// What are the different operation names in our data?

AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(7d)
| distinct OperationName

// This shows you every unique operation name — helpful for understanding
// what your Foundry application is doing.


// ============================================================================
// RECAP — KQL Operators You Learned:
//
//   take N          → Grab N rows (great for quick exploration)
//   where           → Filter rows based on conditions
//   project         → Choose which columns to display
//   sort by         → Order results (asc or desc)
//   count           → Count the number of matching rows
//   extend          → Add a new calculated column
//   distinct        → Show unique values
//   ago()           → Time function (e.g., ago(1h), ago(7d))
//
// NEXT: Explore AppDependencies to analyze
//       the AI model calls that Foundry makes.
// ============================================================================
